// `cat`, in Exsecutor: every byte of standard input, unchanged, on standard
// output. `Lector.ab_introitu(a)` is the reader `Scriptor.ad_exitum(a)` is the
// writer -- spec 4.6 puts all three streams in `ambitus` -- and
// `l.lege_octeto()` is `s.scribe_octeto(b)` turned round.
//
// WHY IT IS A `u16`. A byte has 256 values and the loop needs a 257th thing
// to mean "the input ended", so `lege_octeto` answers 0..255 or 256. That is
// provisional exactly as `scribe`'s count is (spec 11 wants `eventus`, which
// has no syntax yet), and it is why the comparison below is against 256 and
// the write takes `c sicut u8`: inside the loop the value IS a byte.
//
// EOF AND ERROR ARE THE SAME ANSWER. A broken stream also reads 256, so this
// program cannot tell a truncated input from a complete one. Stated here
// because a `cat` that silently stops at an I/O error is a real hazard; the
// fix is `eventus`, not a second sentinel.
//
// THIS COPY READS A LARGE STREAM. tests/programs/lector is `cat` over 41
// bytes, which a one-byte-per-syscall runtime and a buffered one pass alike.
// This is the same program, unchanged below the comment, over
// tests/data/alveus_legendi.bin: 135,169 bytes, byte i being
// `(i ^ (i >> 8) ^ (i >> 16)) & 0xff`. That is two whole 64 KiB read
// buffers and a short third. On the way out the write buffer is drained
// before every refill (a read may block, so nothing written may wait behind
// it) -- which is also what delivers the tail here: the read that finds end
// of input drains it, so the EXIT drain has nothing left to do in this
// program. The exit drain is guarded by every write-only fixture
// (tests/programs/octeti loses 25 of its 26 bytes without it -- measured by
// removing it), and the abort drain by tests/programs/abortus_post_scripturam
// (docs/design/runtime.md 2.4, as amended, lists all five flush points).
// A block lost, doubled or reordered at any boundary shows
// as a `cmp` difference, and the `i >> 16` term makes even a whole lost
// 64 KiB block visible. stdout is compared against the input file itself,
// as tests/programs/lector's is.
//
// `lege` is `emitte`'s shape in tests/programs/octeti: the authority is the
// reader's own (spec 4.2's table -- a capability-bearing type's row is its
// mark), so its declared row is `poscit sicut l` and nothing else.

functio lege(l: Lector) -> u16 poscit sicut l {
    redde l.lege_octeto();
}

publica functio initium(m: Mundus) -> u8 {
    firma a = m.ambitus();
    sub ambitus = a;
    firma l = Lector.ab_introitu(a);
    firma s = Scriptor.ad_exitum(a);

    mutabilis c: u16 = lege(l);
    dum c ne 256 {
        si s.scribe_octeto(c sicut u8) ne 1 {
            redde 2;
        }
        c = lege(l);
    }
    redde 0;
}
